  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Maine received $2.9B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. The Department of Defense obligated $2.8B, making the DoD the dominant buyer in the state. The state recorded 7,791 awards with an average award size of $377K. Obligations fell 25.2% year over year. Ship Building and Repairing is the largest industry by obligations at $2.1B, and Bath Iron Works Corporation is the largest vendor at $2.1B. Direct Health and Medical Insurance Carriers account for $433M in obligations, and MARTINS POINT HEALTH CARE INC accounts for $433M in vendor obligations. Maine’s fiscal-year obligations trended from $2.4B in FY21 to $3.9B in FY25 before the FY26 to date total of $2.9B. Other significant buying agencies include the Department of Labor at $28.5M and the Department of Veterans Affairs at $25.5M. Construction and engineering-related industries together represent multiple entries in the top industry list, with Other Heavy and Civil Engineering Construction at $115M and Engineering Services at $37.6M.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How concentrated are federal obligations in Maine?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense obligated $2.8B, concentrating the bulk of spending in the state. Bath Iron Works Corporation received $2.1B, and Ship Building and Repairing accounted for $2.1B. The top buyer and top vendor totals together explain a large share of the $2.9B in FY2026 to date obligations."
      },
      {
        "heading": "What changed in FY2026 to date versus prior years?",
        "body": "Obligations fell 25.2% year over year. Maine’s FY26 to date total of $2.9B is below the FY25 total of $3.9B and below the FY23 and FY24 totals of $3.7B. The number of awards and average award size together show the market included 7,791 awards at an average award size of $377K."
      }
    ]
  },
